{#
    The scoped layout rules the purchase ENTRY forms share (#655, queue items 15 and 45).

    These lived inline at the bottom of `purchase_order_edit.html.twig`, which made them a property
    of that one screen rather than of the markup they style — and `_purchase_charge_row.html.twig`
    is a SHARED partial whose `.purchase-charge-*` classes were defined only in that one template.
    So any second form including the charge row rendered it unstyled, which is exactly what the
    vendor bill form needed to do. Moving them here fixes that at the root instead of copying the
    block a second time.

    Nothing here is a COLOUR decision and nothing here is a trait. The owner's ruling of 2026-09-12
    is that core CSS owns the trait vocabulary — 'late' looks the same everywhere — and a bundle
    never invents one locally. These are widths, gaps and a rule under the grand-total row: layout
    for a form that core has never seen, carried beside the markup it belongs to. Status, money and
    every other stateful thing on these screens uses core's own classes (`order-status`, `button`,
    `flash`, `table-card`), not one of these.

    Included, never duplicated: one `<style>` per page, and the two forms are two pages.
#}
<style>
    .purchase-charge-bar { display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-end; margin-bottom: 14px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    /* A Main Line fee's row lands directly under the table now (sellDocChargeRowAnchor() in
       app.js, targeting _purchase_lines_table.html.twig's own mainline-fee-anchor marker — the
       buy-side twin of sales_lines_table.html.twig's identical marker). Matches app.css's own
       `.wide-table-wrap > .order-charge-line-row` rule for the same row, scoped here to the first
       .purchase-charge-row directly after the table rather than every one — Before Tax/After Tax
       rows (also .purchase-charge-row, anchored down by the totals box) must not pick this up. */
    table.po-lines-table + .purchase-charge-row,
    table.bill-lines-table + .purchase-charge-row {
        padding: 8px 12px;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--line-light);
        background: #f8fafc;
    }
    table.po-lines-table + .purchase-charge-row > span,
    table.bill-lines-table + .purchase-charge-row > span {
        justify-content: flex-start;
    }
    /* Matches .order-lines-toolbar .order-add-line-bar exactly (public/assets/css/app.css) —
       #full-parity, 2026-09-15: without `width: auto` here, this bar was allocated a fixed share
       of the toolbar's flex row instead of shrinking to its own content, so its own selects wrapped
       onto extra lines below Add Product/Add Blank Line instead of sitting beside them. */
    .order-lines-toolbar .purchase-charge-bar { margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0; width: auto; }
    /* Matches .order-lines-toolbar .order-add-line-bar select exactly — a global `select { width:
       100% }` rule (app.css) is what actually stretched each select to its container's full width;
       `width: auto` on the wrapper alone doesn't touch that, since it's the SELECTS that were
       filling whatever width they were given, not the wrapper collapsing around them. */
    .order-lines-toolbar .purchase-charge-bar select { width: auto; min-width: 150px; }
    /* Matches .order-total-box/.order-total-box div exactly (public/assets/css/app.css) —
       #full-parity, 2026-09-15, corrected: this box used to be independently-authored flex rows
       with no max-width or right-alignment, which is why it rendered full-width on the left
       instead of the sell side's fixed-width box docked to the right of the line table. */
    .purchase-total-box { display: grid; gap: 8px; width: min(430px, 100%); margin: 28px 0 0 auto; color: #000; font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 700; }
    .purchase-total-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 120px; gap: 12px; align-items: center; }
    .purchase-total-row span { text-align: right; }
    .purchase-total-row.grand { border-top: 2px solid #334155; padding-top: 6px; font-size: 1.1em; }
    .purchase-charge-row { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 8px; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .purchase-charge-row > span { display: flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .purchase-charge-label { width: 180px; }
    .purchase-charge-slug { width: 120px; }
    .purchase-charge-select { width: auto; }
    .purchase-charge-amount { width: 110px; }
    .po-line-tax, .po-line-subtotal { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }

    /* Batch/lot capture (#full-parity, 2026-09-13) — same layout as the sell side's
       .order-batch-cell/.order-batch-row (public/assets/css/app.css), scoped here under the
       po-/bill- prefixed classes app.js's generic batch functions key off.

       NO unconditional `.js-only { display: none }` rule here (#full-parity, 2026-09-15,
       corrected: one used to live in this exact spot, claiming to "mirror the sell side's own
       no-JS progressive-enhancement rule" while doing the opposite of it). The sell side's real
       rule lives ONLY inside <noscript> (commercial_document_edit.html.twig's own
       no_js_noscript_style block, which this page already includes) — .js-only elements are
       VISIBLE by default and hidden only when scripting is actually off. An unconditional rule
       here hid every .js-only control (Add Product, Add Blank Line, the charge category filter)
       permanently, JS or not — the opposite of "hidden until scripting runs". #}
    .po-batch-cell, .bill-batch-cell { vertical-align: top; padding: 10px 6px; }
    .po-batch-row, .bill-batch-row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 4px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
    .po-batch-row .js-batch-label, .bill-batch-row .js-batch-label {
        width: 80px; flex-shrink: 0; min-height: 26px; height: 26px; padding: 2px 4px;
        font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.1; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px;
        background-color: #fff; color: var(--ink); box-sizing: border-box;
    }
    .po-batch-row .js-batch-date, .bill-batch-row .js-batch-date {
        flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 0; min-height: 26px; height: 26px; padding: 2px 4px;
        font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.1; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px;
        background-color: #fff; color: var(--ink); box-sizing: border-box;
    }
</style>
